@attribute [Route("/components/" + nameof(HxGoogleTagManager))]
@attribute [Route("/components/" + nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker))]
@using Havit.Blazor.GoogleTagManager

<ApiDoc Type="typeof(HxGoogleTagManager)">
    <DocAlert Type="DocAlertType.Warning">
        <code>HxGoogleTagManager</code> and related components are distributed in a separate NuGet package: <a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Havit.Blazor.GoogleTagManager">Havit.Blazor.GoogleTagManager</a>.
    </DocAlert>

	<DocHeading Title="Basic usage" />
	<p>Install GTM support through application startup:</p>
	<CodeSnippet File="~\Pages\Components\HxGoogleTagManagerDoc\HxGoogleTagManager_ServiceRegistration.CodeSnippet.cs" />
</ApiDoc>

<ApiDoc Type="typeof(HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker)">
    <DocHeading Title="Basic usage" Id="HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker-basic-usage" />
	<p>Install GTM support through application startup (see above) and add <code>HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker</code> to <code>App.razor</code> or <code>MainLayout.razor</code></p>
	<CodeSnippet Language="html">@("<HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker />")</CodeSnippet>

	<DocHeading Title="Render modes" Id="HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker-render-modes" Level="3" />
	<p>All render modes are supported. What the component does depends on where it is rendered:</p>
	<table class="table table-bordered w-auto">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th>Render mode</th>
				<th>How GTM is started and page-views tracked</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td>Static SSR, prerendering</td>
				<td>The GTM snippet is rendered inline, so GTM starts loading while the page is still being parsed.</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>Static SSR with <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/fundamentals/navigation#enhanced-navigation-and-form-handling">enhanced navigation</a></td>
				<td>
					A <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/fundamentals/startup#javascript-initializers">JS initializer</a>
					shipped with the package listens for <code>enhancedload</code>. Blazor loads it automatically, nothing needs to be referenced.
				</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>Interactive Server, WebAssembly, Auto</td>
				<td>Page-views are pushed in reaction to <code>NavigationManager.LocationChanged</code>, which covers navigations resolved by the interactive router.</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<DocHeading Title="Where to place the component" Id="HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker-placement" Level="3" />
	<p>
		In a Blazor Web App the component takes the render mode of the position it is rendered in, and that decides
		which of the rows above applies to it:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<code>App.razor</code> is always statically rendered, which gives the earliest possible GTM load and covers
			enhanced navigation — but not navigations performed by an interactive router.
		</li>
		<li>
			A layout or page inside an interactive island covers interactive routing. With
			<a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/components/render-modes#static-ssr-pages-in-an-interactive-app">global interactivity</a>
			this placement covers the whole app.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Rendering the component more than once (for example in both positions) is safe. Initialization and automatic
		page-views are deduplicated in JavaScript, so a navigation seen by both paths still produces a single event.
	</p>

	<DocHeading Title="Events pushed to the data-layer" Id="HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker-events" Level="3" />
	<table class="table table-bordered w-auto">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th>Event</th>
				<th>When</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td><code>gtm.js</code></td>
				<td>Once per document load, from the GTM snippet.</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>pageview</code></td>
				<td>Once per document load. Not part of Google's snippet — pushed for backwards compatibility.</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>@nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerOptions.PageViewEventName)</code><br />(<code>virtualPageView</code> by default)</td>
				<td>Once per navigation, enhanced navigation and interactive routing included. Configure a trigger for it in your GTM container — <code>pageview</code> alone fires only on real page loads.</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		By default the page the document was loaded with is announced by both <code>pageview</code> and
		<code>@nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerOptions.PageViewEventName)</code>. Which convention your container follows decides
		whether that is what you want:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<code>@nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerOptions.PageViewEventName)</code> is the single source of truth and <code>pageview</code>
			is ignored — keep <code>@nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerOptions.EnableInitialPageViewTracking)</code> at its default <code>true</code>.
		</li>
		<li>
			<code>pageview</code> (or the container's built-in Page View trigger) covers real document loads and
			<code>@nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerOptions.PageViewEventName)</code> only the navigations that follow — set
			<code>@nameof(HxGoogleTagManagerOptions.EnableInitialPageViewTracking)</code> to <code>false</code>, otherwise the
			landing page of every session is counted twice.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<DocHeading Title="Content Security Policy" Id="HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker-csp" Level="3" />
	<p>When a Content Security Policy with <code>nonce-*</code> is in use, pass the nonce to the inline snippet:</p>
	<CodeSnippet Language="html">@("<HxGoogleTagManagerPageViewTracker Nonce=\"@Nonce\" />")</CodeSnippet>
</ApiDoc>
